# Trivy ignore list for the weekly Security scan (issue #21). Every entry
# below is verified upstream-blocked as of 2026-09-15: not fixable by a change
# in this repo. Do not add a CVE here without the same verification: confirm
# which artifact it lives in and that no newer upstream release carries the
# fix. Keeping the list to exactly that keeps a red run informative.
#
# `Image scan (trivy)` runs with `ignore-unfixed: true`, so every finding it
# reports already has a fix somewhere. Before suppressing one, check the two
# things that have so far always been the real answer instead:
#
# - A stale layer. A Debian finding is the pinned base layer being older than
#   bookworm's security archive. The base digest can be current and the
#   packages still old, because the `node:26-bookworm-slim` tag is rebuilt on
#   Node's cadence while Debian publishes as soon as it builds. The runtime
#   stage's `apt-get upgrade` is what clears that (issue #363); see the note at
#   that line.
# - A cached layer. Docker keys a layer on the RUN command's text, not on what
#   apt or npm resolved, so a rebuild can ship the old package and leave the
#   finding in place. publish-image.yml's Sunday cron builds with `no-cache`
#   and pushes the `:edge` image this scan reads on Monday.
#
# Revisit policy: re-check this list whenever gh, npm or the base image move.
# Re-run the scan WITHOUT the ignore file and prune any entry that no longer
# appears. Trivy reads `.trivyignore` from the working directory by default, so
# omitting `--ignorefile` does not disable it and a check run that way reports
# every entry here as already gone. Run it from somewhere else:
#
#   (cd /tmp && trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --ignore-unfixed \
#      ghcr.io/calandria-dev/calandria:edge)
#
# --- PRUNED 2026-09-15 ---
# CVE-2026-56864 and CVE-2026-56865, golang.org/x/mod compiled into
# /usr/bin/gh, were listed here from 2026-08-23. They were blocked on cli/cli's
# own go.mod reaching x/mod v0.40.0. The `gh=` pin is now 2.101.0 and a scan of
# a locally built image with no ignore file reports /usr/bin/gh with no
# findings, so both are gone.

# --- npm's own vendored node_modules, bundled inside npm 12.0.2 ---
# npm 12.0.2 is still npm's newest release (`dist-tags.latest` and `next-12`
# both point at it, checked 2026-09-15), and it vendors brace-expansion 5.0.7,
# tar 7.5.19 and ip-address 10.2.0 in its own bundled node_modules. None of
# this is Calandria's dependency tree: package-lock.json already resolves the
# app's own ip-address to 10.4.0 (above the 10.3.1 fix for CVE-2026-69192,
# confirmed via `npm ls ip-address --all`), so the ip-address hit below is
# npm's bundled copy. The Dockerfile pins `npm install -g npm@12.0.2`; bumping
# that pin is how these clear.
# Watch: https://www.npmjs.com/package/npm - a release past 12.0.2.
# Verified 2026-09-15 against a locally built image.
CVE-2026-14257
CVE-2026-69152
CVE-2026-73566
CVE-2026-69192
